163
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:57,820
We're going to explore how professionals talk about salary naturally and confidently

164
00:09:57,820 --> 00:10:00,060
in English-speaking workplaces.

165
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:05,260
By the end of this episode, you'll have practical phrases you can use immediately, and you'll

166
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:10,620
understand the mindset behind professional salary conversations.

167
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:13,740
Before we begin, think about your own experience.

168
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:18,700
Maybe you avoided asking about salary because you felt nervous, or maybe you translated

169
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:22,980
directly from your native language and it didn't sound quite right.

170
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:24,980
That happens all the time.

171
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:29,300
One of the biggest mistakes learners make is translating directly.

172
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:34,620
Even if the grammar is correct, the tone can sound too strong or too personal.

173
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:41,340
Yes, because in many English-speaking workplaces, salary discussions are usually indirect.

174
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:44,640
People rarely talk about money in a very blunt way.

175
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:50,600
For example, asking a colleague, how much do you earn might feel normal in some cultures,

176
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:55,120
But in many professional English environments, it can feel too private.

177
00:10:55,120 --> 00:11:01,520
Instead, professionals often focus on topics like growth, responsibilities, or compensation,

178
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:03,920
rather than just saying money.

179
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:09,000
So instead of saying, I want more money, a more natural phrase might be, I'd like to

180
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,440
discuss my compensation.

181
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:14,720
That sounds much more professional and less emotional.

182
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:17,440
Another example is during job interviews.

183
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:22,480
Some learners say, how much will you pay me, which sounds a little direct.

184
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:26,760
A better option is, could you share the salary range for this position?

185
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:31,080
It sounds respectful and shows you understand professional communication.

186
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:34,800
Let's talk about why wording matters so much.

187
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:39,160
Salary conversations are not just about information, they're about relationships

188
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:41,040
and professionalism.

189
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:43,920
Yes, language reflects attitude.

190
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:48,100
If your words sound demanding, people may assume you're difficult to work with, even

191
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:49,700
if that's not true.

192
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:55,980
And if you sound too indirect or unsure, you might not communicate your needs clearly.

193
00:11:55,980 --> 00:12:00,060
So the goal is balance, polite but confident.

194
00:12:00,060 --> 00:12:01,060
Exactly.

195
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:06,540
Now, let's look at some useful vocabulary because many learners get confused by similar

196
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:07,540
words.

197
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:14,000
Salary usually means a fixed amount of money paid regularly, often monthly or yearly.

198
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,320
Wages typically refer to hourly pay.

199
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:23,760
And compensation is a broader professional term that includes salary plus benefits.

200
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:29,360
Benefits can include health insurance, vacation days, bonuses, flexible working hours, or

201
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:31,480
training opportunities.

202
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:36,440
So when someone asks about the compensation package, they are asking about the complete

203
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:39,280
offer, not just the salary number.

204
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:43,680
Another important difference is between a raise and a promotion.

205
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:46,680
A raise means more money, but the same role.

206
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:52,200
A promotion means new responsibilities or a higher position, which may include more

207
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:53,200
money.

208
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,560
Understanding these differences helps you sound more professional and precise.

209
00:12:57,560 --> 00:12:59,800
Let's imagine a real-life situation.

210
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:04,120
You're working in a company, and after one year, you feel ready to talk about

211
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:06,000
salary growth.

212
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,320
Many learners might say, I need more money, but that sounds emotional and personal.

213
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:17,420
Instead, you could say, I'd like to review my compensation based on my recent contributions.

214
00:13:17,420 --> 00:13:20,960
Notice how that focuses on value rather than need.

215
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:26,480
Yes, professional English often focuses on results and performance.

216
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:31,220
Another good phrase is, I've taken on additional responsibilities recently and I was hoping

217
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:33,380
we could revisit my salary.

218
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:37,620
That sounds respectful and confident at the same time.

219
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:42,900
Let's talk about tone for a moment because tone is just as important as vocabulary.

220
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:44,160
Absolutely.

221
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:49,580
Even perfect grammar can sound wrong if the tone feels too strong.

222
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:52,180
Softening phrases are extremely useful.

223
00:13:52,180 --> 00:13:56,760
For example, I was wondering if, or would it be possible to?

224
00:13:56,760 --> 00:14:01,500
These phrases reduce pressure and make your requests sound collaborative.

225
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:03,000
Here's another tip.

226
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,400
comparing yourself directly to co-workers. Yes, saying something like,

227
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:11,800
my co-worker earns more than me can sound negative or confrontational.

228
00:14:12,440 --> 00:14:17,800
Instead, focus on your own achievements. For example, based on my performance and

229
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:21,000
contributions, I'd like to discuss a salary adjustment.

230
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:27,720
Let's also talk about common mistakes. Some learners say, I gain salary. But in English,

231
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:30,960
we say, I earn or I make.

232
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:34,000
Another mistake is saying expensive salary.

233
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,020
We say high salary or competitive salary.

234
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:42,560
And remember, money is uncountable, so we don't say a-money.

235
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:46,360
Now let's imagine another scenario, a job interview.

236
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:49,720
When is the right time to ask about salary?

237
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:54,360
Usually after you understand the role and responsibilities, asking too early may make

238
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,840
it seem like you're only interested in money.

239
00:14:56,840 --> 00:14:59,000
A professional way to ask could be,

240
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,000
could you tell me more about the compensation structure for this role?

241
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,600
That sounds professional and shows awareness of business culture.

242
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:08,040
Here's something important.

243
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:11,160
Confidence doesn't mean being aggressive.

244
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:15,000
Confidence means speaking clearly and respectfully.

245
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,240
Yes, and preparation helps.

246
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:21,560
